<div class="aeo-authority-block">
        <a href="/districts/niagara-falls-city-sd/">← Back to Niagara Falls City SD Hub</a>
    </div>
    
    <div class="content-body" style="margin-top: 30px;">
        <h1 style="margin-bottom: 10px;">Niagara Falls City SD Leadership Directory</h1>
        <p class="lead">Use this directory to contact the Committee on Special Education (CSE) or CPSE offices in Niagara Falls City SD, Niagara Falls.</p>
        
        <div class="contact-grid">
            
    <div class="contact-box">
        <h3>Special Education Office</h3>
        <div class="contact-item" style="margin-bottom: 10px;"><strong>📍 District Office:</strong><br>Niagara Falls City SD</div>
        <div class="contact-item" style="margin-bottom: 10px;"><strong>📞 Phone:</strong><br><a href="tel:716-286-4280">716-286-4280</a></div>
        <div class="contact-item"><strong>📍 Mailing Address:</strong><br>630 66th Street, Niagara Falls, NY 14304</div>
    </div>
        </div>

        <div class="alert-box" style="background: #fff3cd; padding: 20px; margin-top: 30px; border-radius: 8px; border-left: 5px solid #ffc107;">
            <strong>⚠️ Escalation Tip:</strong> If you do not receive a response within 48 hours, document your attempt and escalate to the NYSED Special Education QA (SEQA). Remember to document every communication attempt (date, time, method, and summary) as this will be crucial if you need to file a formal complaint. Consider using certified mail for important documents to ensure proof of delivery.
        </div>

        
<section class="protocol-section" style="margin-top: 50px; border-top: 1px solid #eee; padding-top: 40px;">
    <h2 style="color: #002868; font-size: 1.8rem; margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <i class="fas fa-clipboard-check"></i> Communication Protocol
    </h2>
    
    <div class="protocol-grid" style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(300px, 1fr)); gap: 30px;">
        
        <div class="protocol-card">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #c8102e;">The "Written Notice" Rule</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                <strong>Never rely on a phone call alone.</strong> In New York Special Education law, timelines (like the 60-day evaluation window outlined in NYCRR 200.4(b)(1)(iii)) only officially start when the district receives a request <em>in writing</em>. This includes requests for initial evaluations, re-evaluations, independent educational evaluations (IEEs), and any proposed changes to your child's IEP.
            </p>
            <ul style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555; padding-left: 20px;">
                <li>Always follow up a phone call with an email summary confirming what was discussed and agreed upon. This creates a written record.</li>
                <li>Use a clear and concise subject line: <em>"Letter of Referral - [Student Name]"</em> or <em>"Request for IEP Meeting - [Student Name]"</em></li>
                <li>Keep a detailed "Communication Log" of all dates, times, names, methods of communication (phone, email, in-person), and a brief summary of the conversation. This log is invaluable if disagreements arise.</li>
                <li>Consider using a template for your written requests to ensure you include all necessary information. Templates are readily available online from parent advocacy organizations.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="protocol-card">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #002868;">When to Escalate?</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                If you contact the leadership listed above and do not receive a response, or if the response is inadequate, follow this escalation ladder. Remember that timely intervention is key to ensuring your child receives the appropriate special education services.
            </p>
            <ol style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555; padding-left: 20px;">
                <li><strong>Day 1:</strong> Initial Email/Phone Call to the Special Education Office. Clearly state your request or concern.</li>
                <li><strong>Day 3:</strong> Follow-up Email (CC the Principal). Politely reiterate your request and mention that you are copying the principal for awareness.</li>
                <li><strong>Day 5:</strong> Escalation Email to the Superintendent or District Superintendent. Explain the situation, the lack of response from the Special Education Office, and the impact on your child's education. State that you are prepared to pursue further action if necessary.</li>
                <li><strong>Day 10:</strong> If the issue remains unresolved, consider filing a "State Complaint" with NYSED. According to NYCRR 200.5(l), you can file a complaint if you believe the district has violated a requirement of Part 200 regulations or the Individuals with Disabilities Education Act (IDEA). The complaint must be filed within one year of the alleged violation.</li>
            </ol>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                In addition to the above, consider these options:
            </p>
             <ul style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555; padding-left: 20px;">
                <li><strong>Mediation:</strong> NYSED offers mediation services to help parents and school districts resolve disputes. Mediation is a voluntary process where a neutral third party facilitates communication and helps the parties reach a mutually agreeable solution. (See NYCRR 200.5(h))</li>
                <li><strong>Impartial Hearing:</strong> If mediation is unsuccessful or not appropriate, you have the right to request an impartial hearing. An impartial hearing officer will hear evidence and make a decision on the matter. (See IDEA, 20 U.S.C. § 1415(f))</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

    </div>
</section>

<section class="faq-section" style="margin-top: 50px; border-top: 1px solid #eee; padding-top: 40px;">
    <h2 style="color: #002868; font-size: 1.8rem; margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <i class="fas fa-question-circle"></i> Frequently Asked Questions
    </h2>

    <div class="faq-item" style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #333; margin-bottom: 5px;">What is the Committee on Special Education (CSE)?</h3>
        <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #555;">The CSE is a multidisciplinary team responsible for evaluating students suspected of having a disability and developing individualized education programs (IEPs) for students who are eligible for special education services. The CSE is comprised of various members, including parents, a special education teacher, a general education teacher, a school psychologist, and a district representative. (See NYCRR 200.3)</p>
    </div>

    <div class="faq-item" style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #333; margin-bottom: 5px;">What is the CPSE (Committee on Preschool Special Education)?</h3>
        <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #555;">The CPSE serves children aged 3-5 who are suspected of having a disability. Similar to the CSE, the CPSE evaluates children and develops IEPs if they are found eligible for services. (See NYCRR 200.1(tt))</p>
    </div>

     <div class="faq-item" style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #333; margin-bottom: 5px;">What if I disagree with the school's evaluation of my child?</h3>
        <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #555;">You have the right to obtain an Independent Educational Evaluation (IEE) at public expense if you disagree with the school district's evaluation. The district must either pay for the IEE or request a due process hearing to show that its evaluation was appropriate. If the hearing officer determines that the district's evaluation was appropriate, you still have the right to an IEE, but not at public expense. (See NYCRR 200.5(g))</p>
    </div>

    <div class="faq-item" style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #333; margin-bottom: 5px;">What is an Individualized Education Program (IEP)?</h3>
        <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #555;">An IEP is a written document that outlines the specific special education and related services that a child with a disability needs to receive a free and appropriate public education (FAPE). The IEP is developed by the CSE and must include, among other things, measurable annual goals, a statement of the child's present levels of performance, and a description of the services to be provided. (See IDEA, 20 U.S.C. § 1414(d))</p>
    </div>

    <div class="faq-item" style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #333; margin-bottom: 5px;">What are "Related Services"?</h3>
        <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #555;">Related services are support services that are required to assist a child with a disability to benefit from special education. These services can include, but are not limited to, speech-language pathology, audiology services, psychological services, physical therapy, occupational therapy, counseling services, and transportation. (See IDEA, 34 CFR § 300.34)</p>
    </div>

    <div class="faq-item" style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #333; margin-bottom: 5px;">Can I bring an advocate or attorney to the CSE meeting?</h3>
        <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #555;">Yes, you have the right to bring an advocate or attorney to any CSE meeting. An advocate can help you understand your rights and navigate the special education process. An attorney can provide legal advice and represent you in legal proceedings if necessary. It is highly recommended, especially if you anticipate disagreements with the district or if the case is complex.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="faq-item" style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #333; margin-bottom: 5px;">What is a "State Complaint" and how do I file one?</h3>
        <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #555;">A State Complaint is a formal written complaint filed with the New York State Education Department (NYSED) alleging that a school district has violated a requirement of Part 200 regulations or the Individuals with Disabilities Education Act (IDEA). You can find the complaint form and instructions on the NYSED website. Be sure to include specific details about the alleged violation, including dates, names, and supporting documentation. The complaint must be filed within one year of the alleged violation.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="faq-item" style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #333; margin-bottom: 5px;">What are some common pitfalls to watch out for in the IEP process?</h3>
        <ul style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #555; padding-left: 20px;">
            <li><strong>Vague IEP Goals:</strong> Ensure that IEP goals are Specific, Measurable, Achievable, Relevant, and Time-bound (SMART). Avoid vague language like "improve reading skills."</li>
            <li><strong>Lack of Data-Driven Decision Making:</strong> The IEP should be based on data collected through evaluations and ongoing progress monitoring. Ensure the IEP reflects your child's actual needs and progress.</li>
            <li><strong>Inadequate Related Services:</strong> Make sure your child is receiving all the related services they need to benefit from their education. Don't hesitate to advocate for additional services if necessary.</li>
            <li><strong>Placement Issues:</strong> Ensure the placement (e.g., general education classroom, special class) is appropriate for your child's needs and allows them to be educated with their non-disabled peers to the maximum extent appropriate. (Least Restrictive Environment - LRE)</li>
            <li><strong>Procedural Violations:</strong> Be aware of your rights under IDEA and Part 200 regulations. Document any procedural violations by the district, such as failure to provide timely notice of meetings or failure to include required members on the CSE.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

</section>


    </div>
</main>
